# Do Work

## Workflow

### 1. Plan

Before writing any code:

- Clarify scope — restate the task in one sentence and confirm with the user if ambiguous
- Identify affected files by reading relevant parts of the codebase
- List the steps you will take (ordered, specific)
- Flag risks or unknowns upfront

### 2. Implement

Work through your plan step by step:

- Make one logical change at a time
- Follow existing code conventions (naming, structure, patterns)
- Do not add features, abstractions, or refactors beyond what was asked

### 3. Feedback loop

After each meaningful chunk of implementation, run:

```bash
pnpm run type-check
```

```bash
pnpm run test
```

- Fix all type errors before moving to the next step
- Fix failing tests before moving to the next step
- Do not proceed with known failures unless explicitly asked

### 4. Completion

When all checks pass:

- Summarize what changed and why (one sentence per file)
- Call out anything the user should verify manually
- Do not commit unless asked

## Rules

- Always read files before editing them
- If type-check or tests fail after a fix, diagnose before retrying — do not blindly iterate
- If a test was already failing before your changes, note it rather than treating it as your bug
- Ask the user before making changes outside the originally scoped files
